      "diagnosis": "A consistent optimality gap can arise from an incorrect DC network model (Bbus construction, handling of transformer taps/phase shifters, sign conventions, or mapping buses), which changes feasible set and dispatch cost. The task depends on accurate line-flow constraints and susceptance modeling, so any mismatch to MATPOWER DC conventions can produce a higher-than-optimal solution relative to the verifier.",
      "repair_instruction": "Expand and complete the truncated MATPOWER DC conventions: (1) provide full formulas for Bbus and branch flow including TAP (tau) and SHIFT (degrees->radians) and how they enter injections/flows, (2) specify correct line flow expression used for thermal constraints (from-end flow with tap), (3) emphasize bus-number mapping for non-contiguous buses, and (4) include a minimal validation procedure comparing computed flows/Bbus to MATPOWER 'makeBdc' outputs (or equivalent) for a small case."
